WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.700
Inside your cells, something acts like a separate living thing.

2
00:00:04.540 --> 00:00:08.960
Your mitochondria keep their own DNA, apart from the DNA in your nucleus.

3
00:00:09.880 --> 00:00:11.780
And they multiply by pinching in two.

4
00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:13.960
Why would a part of you do that?

5
00:00:17.366 --> 00:00:19.526
The answer is a very old story.

6
00:00:20.426 --> 00:00:22.866
Eukaryotes are living things with cells like ours.

7
00:00:23.806 --> 00:00:28.746
Their oldest widely accepted fossils are more than 1.5 billion years old.

8
00:00:29.800 --> 00:00:32.340
Exactly when this story began is uncertain.

9
00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:36.820
But rewind far enough, and the cell comes apart into two strangers.

10
00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:39.340
Meet the first stranger.

11
00:00:40.120 --> 00:00:43.960
It was most likely an archaeon, a kind of single-celled microbe.

12
00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:47.060
Whether it already had a nucleus is still debated.

13
00:00:48.283 --> 00:00:53.643
The second stranger was a bacterium, related to today's alphaproteobacteria.

14
00:00:54.503 --> 00:00:55.943
It could probably respire.

15
00:00:56.723 --> 00:01:00.003
That means a chain in its membrane pulled energy out of food.

16
00:01:00.783 --> 00:01:02.663
And it may have used oxygen to do it.

17
00:01:03.616 --> 00:01:05.056
Those are long names.

18
00:01:05.976 --> 00:01:07.596
Just call them the host and the guest.

19
00:01:08.476 --> 00:01:10.796
Somehow, the host came to enclose the guest.

20
00:01:11.596 --> 00:01:13.156
What do you expect will happen next?

21
00:01:18.683 --> 00:01:20.103
You might expect a meal.

22
00:01:20.703 --> 00:01:23.343
But instead of being digested, the guest stayed.

23
00:01:24.243 --> 00:01:25.943
Two strangers now lived as one.

24
00:01:26.983 --> 00:01:28.203
Here is the claim.

25
00:01:29.083 --> 00:01:31.343
Your mitochondria descend from that guest.

26
00:01:32.183 --> 00:01:34.483
But how could anyone know, so long afterwards?

27
00:01:35.383 --> 00:01:38.123
If a bacterium moved in, what would it leave behind?

28
00:01:43.700 --> 00:01:47.860
Here they are. Most mitochondria still have two membranes.

29
00:01:48.840 --> 00:01:49.940
They have their own DNA.

30
00:01:50.920 --> 00:01:54.140
Their ribosomes, which build proteins, are bacteria-like.

31
00:01:54.960 --> 00:01:56.720
And they divide by pinching in two.

32
00:01:57.750 --> 00:01:59.650
One more clue comes from genes.

33
00:02:00.630 --> 00:02:05.810
On the family tree, mitochondria from animals, plants and fungi all sit on one

34
00:02:05.810 --> 00:02:09.090
branch. That suggests this happened only once.

35
00:02:09.930 --> 00:02:13.290
Every mitochondrion today descends from that single guest.

36
00:02:14.350 --> 00:02:16.290
So here is the idea to keep.

37
00:02:16.970 --> 00:02:19.890
A mitochondrion is not something your cells invented.

38
00:02:20.770 --> 00:02:21.850
It is a guest that stayed.

39
00:02:22.933 --> 00:02:24.833
But why did the partnership begin?

40
00:02:25.713 --> 00:02:26.993
Go back to the two strangers.

41
00:02:27.933 --> 00:02:30.333
The honest answer is that it is still debated.

42
00:02:31.293 --> 00:02:32.373
Here are two of the ideas.

43
00:02:33.516 --> 00:02:37.296
The hydrogen hypothesis says the host lived on hydrogen.

44
00:02:38.136 --> 00:02:39.996
The guest gave off hydrogen as waste.

45
00:02:40.876 --> 00:02:42.816
So the host came to depend on the guest.

46
00:02:43.800 --> 00:02:46.080
The second idea is about protection.

47
00:02:47.080 --> 00:02:50.680
Oxygen can damage cells, and this idea says it harmed the host.

48
00:02:51.540 --> 00:02:54.360
The guest consumed oxygen, and shielded its partner.

49
00:02:55.483 --> 00:02:58.643
Both ideas fit some evidence, and neither is proven.

50
00:02:59.563 --> 00:03:00.803
One hint is real, though.

51
00:03:01.563 --> 00:03:06.863
A close relative of the host, grown in a lab, lives by trading hydrogen with partner

52
00:03:06.863 --> 00:03:10.043
microbes. So partnerships like this do exist.

53
00:03:11.166 --> 00:03:13.306
Whatever began it, the guest changed.

54
00:03:14.186 --> 00:03:17.526
Over time it became an organelle, a working part of the cell.

55
00:03:18.426 --> 00:03:19.826
Many of its genes were lost.

56
00:03:20.906 --> 00:03:22.026
Others moved to the nucleus.

57
00:03:23.150 --> 00:03:25.370
But it kept a small genome of its own.

58
00:03:26.230 --> 00:03:29.750
Human mitochondrial DNA encodes just 13 proteins.

59
00:03:30.730 --> 00:03:36.530
All 13 are for respiration and for making ATP, the molecule that powers your cells.

60
00:03:37.633 --> 00:03:39.013
What did the host gain?

61
00:03:40.093 --> 00:03:42.113
Mitochondria make most of your ATP.

62
00:03:43.053 --> 00:03:46.453
One influential idea, still argued over, goes further.

63
00:03:47.113 --> 00:03:52.693
It says the energy from many mitochondria let cells like ours afford larger genomes

64
00:03:52.693 --> 00:03:54.553
and more complex lives.

65
00:03:55.666 --> 00:04:00.106
So look again. Why would a part of you act like a separate living thing?

66
00:04:00.986 --> 00:04:02.846
Because its ancestor once was one.

67
00:04:03.900 --> 00:04:09.400
Every breath you take delivers oxygen to a chain inherited from that ancient guest.

68
00:04:10.260 --> 00:04:13.100
Your mitochondria began as a guest, and never left.
